{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjWoNmqxtB1HG212ok6o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YU6MXHEscwTtq9PpgMdy4X","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Start: after the clef and three flats come two beamed eighths, Eb3 then D3, and a barline. These are the last two eighths of bar 81 (…Eb3 D3). Bar 79 ends G3 F3, so they don't fit there. The bar before the line started bar 81.\nBar 82: one whole note on Eb3.\nBar 83: a dotted-quarter Eb3, then a run of beamed eighths (D3, then Eb3 D3 Eb3 D3). The copyist's slur and the rf aren't clearly visible.\nBar 84: the copyist abbreviates repeated eighths as half notes with strokes. Two slashed notes, Eb3 then low G2, with a \"dolce\"-like word above.\nBar 85: two slashed notes, Ab2 then Bb2.\nBar 86: a flat, then two slashed notes, Cb3 and Cb3.\nBar 87: two slashed notes, Bb2 and Bb2.\nBar 88: two slashed notes on the Bb2/D3 position. The edition's double stops are not clearly separate heads in the copy.\nBar 89: quarter Eb3, rest, quarter Eb3, rest.\nBar 90: a high quarter Bb3 above the staff, rest, a low quarter Bb2, rest, then the final barline.\nSo the line holds the end of bar 81 through bar 90. Bar 80's whole note is on the previous line.","right":false,"insights":"The start is the hard part. Bars 80–81 and 82–83 are identical, so a whole note followed by an Eb/D bar fits either pair. Only the two pickup eighths (Eb3 D3, which match bar 81 and not bar 79's G3 F3) settle it. The stroke abbreviations make bars 84–88 easy to count. Seeing the end of the line before, or having the edition mark the repeated bar pairs, would have made this easier to decide."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6089,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6089},"out